{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And HaShem turned… and to Cain…</b> The reason Scripture did not begin by stating what preceded on this pattern — ״He did not turn to Cain and He turned to Abel״ — is to teach us the great distance of Cain's removal: that even after there was some satisfaction before HaShem by virtue of Abel's offering, He did not turn to Cain and to his offering, even though he was first in the gift and older in the order of birth — he descended lower and lower." "And the reason Scripture repeated, saying <b>to Abel and to his offering and to Cain and to</b> etc., and did not suffice to say ״to Abel's offering and to Cain's offering״ — here is a hint that the Master would show a countenance of favor to those who brought near their gift, and this is the meaning of His saying <b>and He turned to Abel</b> — He also accepted his gift; <b>and to Cain</b> — He showed no countenance, neither to him nor to his gift."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:11.757164458Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}